        "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14223630/assets/2bbc3e9ce241640af48af50c7d0e0c2c4120b0fc16a00fbc7837ecaeab6bbd9a.png)\n\n \n\nA function $f(x)$ is infinitely differentiable for all real values of its argument $x.$ The graph of $y=f(x)$ and the tangent line to it at the point where $x=0$ are shown above. A few selected derivatives of $f(x)$ at $x=0$ are also given in the table below.\n\n| $n$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{(n)}(0)$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-1$ | $3$ |\n\nIn addition, the function $g$ is defined as \n\n$$\ng(x)={\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFurthermore, it is known that the coefficients of the terms of the Maclaurin series for $g(x)$ form a convergent alternating series $S={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}.$",
